            "label": "compiled dynamics and torque-pulse system identification",
            "module": "robot_learning.lab.compiled_dynamics"
          }
        ],
        "rationale": "The initial scientific distinction is whether the policy problem is primarily action authority/sluggishness or braking and hold stabilization. The artifact records compiled inertial, damping, armature, actuator, timing, and signed unit-pulse responses across extended, folded, intermediate, and rotated postures. If pulses produce low joint/endpoint response or long settling, the first direction should emphasize authority, horizon, and official inner-radius coverage; if response is fast but endpoint motion persists after braking, the first direction should emphasize stabilization-aware learning and hold diagnostics. If behavior varies strongly by posture, geometry-conditioned policy coverage becomes a coupled priority. This directly tests the model's highest-impact physical unknown without changing the protected task."
      },
